Subject: Tolerate disabled signal handling in the daemon

Vela runs signal-cli as a GraalVM native image inside a macOS App Sandbox.
GraalVM implements `sun.misc.Signal` on top of a named POSIX semaphore, and the
sandbox refuses `sem_open` for names that are not prefixed with an application
group identifier. The image therefore aborts at startup with:

    Fatal error: CSunMiscSignal.open() failed.

unless it is launched with `-XX:-EnableSignalHandling`. With that flag set,
`Shutdown.installHandler` instead throws:

    java.lang.IllegalArgumentException: Signal handlers can't be installed if
    signal handling is disabled, see option 'EnableSignalHandling'.

The handlers only exist so an interactive Ctrl-C shuts the daemon down cleanly.
Vela owns the process lifecycle and stops the daemon by terminating it, so their
absence changes nothing for this embedding. The JVM shutdown hook is still
installed, so the ordinary termination path is unaffected.

--- a/src/main/java/org/asamk/signal/Shutdown.java
+++ b/src/main/java/org/asamk/signal/Shutdown.java
@@
         initialized = true;
-        Signal.handle(new Signal("INT"), Shutdown::handleSignal);
-        Signal.handle(new Signal("TERM"), Shutdown::handleSignal);
+        // Signal handling is unavailable when running as a sandboxed GraalVM
+        // native image. The shutdown hook below still runs, so termination is
+        // handled; only the interactive Ctrl-C path is lost.
+        try {
+            Signal.handle(new Signal("INT"), Shutdown::handleSignal);
+            Signal.handle(new Signal("TERM"), Shutdown::handleSignal);
+        } catch (IllegalArgumentException | UnsupportedOperationException e) {
+            logger.debug("Signal handlers unavailable; relying on the shutdown hook.", e);
+        }
         Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(Thread.ofPlatform().unstarted(() -> {
